WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.372
File-handling code needs tests that look like real filesystem work, not only ideal success cases.

2
00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:13.540
Useful scenarios include missing files, empty files, oversized files, malformed files, truncated inputs, read-only directories, permission failures,

3
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.125
unusual encodings, existing destinations, and cleanup after interrupted workflows.

4
00:00:17.125 --> 00:00:24.691
Where practical, include platform-specific path behavior in the test plan instead of assuming every filesystem behaves the same.

5
00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:29.072
Failure cases deserve special attention because they often combine several concerns.

6
00:00:29.072 --> 00:00:33.851
A parser may reject a file after it has opened a handle.

7
00:00:33.851 --> 00:00:37.435
A writer may fail after creating a temporary file.

8
00:00:37.435 --> 00:00:41.418
A replacement may succeed but a later cleanup may not.

9
00:00:41.418 --> 00:00:49.382
Tests should describe the expected state after failure: which files remain, which outputs are trustworthy, and what the application reports.

10
00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:52.967
Logging should support diagnosis without becoming a disclosure channel.

11
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:59.737
Avoid routine logs that include sensitive paths, full file contents, credentials, private data, or internal directory layout.

12
00:00:59.737 --> 00:01:04.118
Prefer structured events with operation categories, safe identifiers, and redacted fields.

13
00:01:04.118 --> 00:01:08.498
User-facing messages and operator diagnostics can have different detail levels.

14
00:01:08.498 --> 00:01:10.888
Code review should ask practical questions.

15
00:01:10.888 --> 00:01:12.481
Which paths are allowed?

16
00:01:12.481 --> 00:01:14.074
Who controls this file?

17
00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:16.861
Are contents validated separately from path policy?

18
00:01:16.861 --> 00:01:18.056
Are permissions narrow?

19
00:01:18.056 --> 00:01:20.844
Can a different process change the target?

20
00:01:20.844 --> 00:01:24.030
Is cleanup reliable across early returns and exceptions?

21
00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.623
Are platform assumptions documented?

22
00:01:25.623 --> 00:01:30.800
A review checklist turns file safety from personal intuition into repeatable engineering practice.
